WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.310
Jack Grapes, Author/Writer/Instructor/Poet/Actor:
And you're not in any creative process at

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:09.540
all I would never tell anybody to outline
your story except there's two exceptions…

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.000
Film Courage: Creativity is a process not
a…

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.010
Jack: Prescription for product.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:24.010
Yeah I don't say you know here's the finished
product let's work to make the product and

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:27.179
a product has this, it has that, it is this,
it is that.

00:00:27.179 --> 00:00:33.890
That's what most writing classes do matter
of fact that's what most literary criticism

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:41.650
in the 19th century did and in the 20th century
literary criticism even shifted to what what

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:48.090
are the techniques or the devices that the
author used not what is his life about and

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:54.440
the freudian implications and the social milieu
of the time but what are the devices that

00:00:54.440 --> 00:01:02.760
the artist use to make that product well I
kind of go further back and go the devices

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:11.620
are something that we write in in the process
toward product but we don't aim for the finished

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:18.430
product we don't know where we're going to
go the creative work is about the process

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:26.090
working on the concepts the exercises not
on a prescription for a finished product so

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:34.640
the concepts I teach in method writing are
not the end product they are the process impulse

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.390
so all the concepts I teach not just in level
one and two that we kind of talked about but

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:45.390
in level three four and five and then the
levels beyond I have a whole bunch of levels

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:53.850
um it's it's about the process I'm always
wanting the artist to honor the process the

00:01:53.850 --> 00:02:00.990
way you work not what you're aiming for but
just let the truth of the impulse of your

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:07.950
process find its way and then when you finally
get your novel or your story or your poem

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:15.430
you won't have headed there and my analogy
in sports is in golf you don't aim you don't

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:22.600
aim to get the ball in the hole you hit the
ball correctly you get the right club and

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:28.120
you swing correctly and if you do that it'll
go where it's supposed to go but you don't

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:35.510
aim to put it in the hole so you you go through
your process and let the end result take care

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:42.200
of itself so that's what I mean when I say
the creativity is about the process matter

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:49.600
of fact isn't that the term they use they
say the creative process so if you come from

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:57.970
the process the product will emerge and it
will always surprise you it will be unexpected

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:01.730
you didn't expect to be stuck.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:09.320
We started out with I'm really thinking about
da da da and by the time we got through going

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:15.250
through some of the techniques of the other
side of the coin and and other things I didn't

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:20.210
bring up you end up being stuck and writing
about that subject that you didn't expect

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:29.420
to write about so that's what I mean by it's
not a prescription for product it's a it's

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:30.730
a process.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:32.220
Does it make sense?

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:33.220
Film Courage: It does.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.810
Do you see a lot of writers that come in with
the product mindset?

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:36.810
Jack: Everybody.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.300
Film Courage: Everybody, you think everybody
starts out that way?

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.300
Jack: Sure, we're all human.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:45.680
We all have you know ideas things we want
to do most people have some kind of idea what

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:53.160
they want to write about and they're always
surprised that in some way they did do it

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:58.770
but they took a route they didn't expect and
if they had gone straight for it, it wouldn't

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:05.990
have been what it ended up being and that
the you know the unexpected path there was

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:09.970
what makes it interesting not where they got.

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:13.750
Film Courage: Why is it important to outline
the story before you start writing?

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:19.680
Jack: It's not important, I would never suggest
anybody do that, that's exactly what I just

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.830
finished talking about that's where you you're
looking at your product and and you know fitting

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:29.780
your feet into the shoes of the right size
and you're not in any creative process at

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:30.780
all.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:36.580
I would never tell anybody to outline your
story except there's two exceptions.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:42.190
You would make an outline of your story if
you're being paid.

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:48.050
And this is the other component because if
you're just being paid and this other component

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:55.620
isn't there then it doesn't apply you're being
paid and there's a deadline tomorrow or at

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.620
the end of the week.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:03.680
Well okay they're paying me they want me to
write a story about ballistic missiles okay

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:10.190
I’ll do the best I can with my talent I’ll
outline what I need to do blah blah blah I’ll

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:14.770
get it done in a week and I’ll make five
thousand dollars but if they're not paying

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:22.720
me and there's no deadline why would I go
with an outline for the product when the creative

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:29.710
process is much richer and holds so many unexpected
marvels.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:35.610
That's what I want to bet on I want to bet
on that horse the unexpected marvel that will

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:41.770
come from the unknown and the unexpected of
the creative process.

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:47.590
Why would I do if no one's paying me and there's
no deadline why would I purposely sabotage

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:55.460
my genius which doesn't come out all the time
anyway if ever I'm not going to make an outline

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:57.620
because then I'm bound by the outline.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:02.190
Film Courage: So you don't need to know where
you're going when you when you're writing

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:03.190
a story?

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:08.970
Jack: It's not about needing to know you are
better off if you don't know if you know you're

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:18.100
probably going to be in trouble it's like
love…I'm not going to fill that blank.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:23.720
Film Courage: Tell me, I want to hear it.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:30.419
Jack: If you have a card and every time with
your date you keep looking at the card already

00:06:30.419 --> 00:06:36.260
say something funny okay you know it's it's
got a list of things you should do that will

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:44.180
make your date want you I mean that's ridiculous
you have to be in the moment and you have

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:49.870
to just be there be in the moment see what
happens love always happens when you don't

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:51.860
expect it to happen, right?

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:55.400
Film Courage: Yeah, that's true that's true.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:57.530
So you don't think blind dates work?

00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:58.530
Jack: What?

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:01.120
Film Courage: You don't think blind dates
work?

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:05.259
Jack: If they're kept blind, if you are open.

00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:13.100
As a matter of fact I'm saying the opposite,
I'm saying blind dates can work.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:17.980
Listen to the stories that people tell of
how they met you know people who've been together

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:22.720
for three four five years at least I’ll
say you know I never expected that he was

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:29.250
my uncle's neighbor and it was a wedding and
he was there and and he's not anything like

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:33.000
the person I thought I would fall in love
with I thought it was going to be this this

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:39.910
and that and I don't know just something happened
and the next thing you know we got five kids.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:47.520
Jack: You can’t…oh there's a…is it The
Supremes [1966].

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.800
You can't something about love what is it?

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.350
It's just very love can’t.

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:56.199
Film Courage: You can't hurry love, you just
have to wait.

00:07:56.199 --> 00:07:58.560
Sorry you don't want me to sing.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:01.760
Jack: No you don't want me to sing it.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:04.300
You I would be happy to hear you.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:07.340
Film Courage: Okay we can we can put a link
to the video for the song.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:11.310
Jack: You know if something is going to be
written it's going to be written don't be

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:15.820
afraid yeah you got that story about you when
you went fishing with your fight it'll it'll

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:23.600
come through don't don't worry about it go
with the unknown and it'll come in when you

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:32.529
least expect it but go with the unknown that's
the creative process unless someone's paying

00:08:32.529 --> 00:08:39.190
you right and there's a deadline well you
know you're not you're not stupid someone's

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:45.240
gonna give me money to write about ballistic
missiles okay i'll do it but otherwise I'm

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:47.089
gonna go with the unknown.

00:08:47.089 --> 00:08:54.139
Film Courage: So what about the people that
say you must have a road map for where you're

00:08:54.139 --> 00:08:57.490
going if not your story will not turn out?

00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:06.249
Jack: And I’ve got a Uranium mine that you
must invest in somewhere out in Nevada.

00:09:06.249 --> 00:09:13.170
You give me ten thousand dollars of your hard-earned
money and I’ll make you a million.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:14.899
What would you say to that?

00:09:14.899 --> 00:09:17.439
Film Courage: Can I go visit the mine?

00:09:17.439 --> 00:09:19.689
Can you take me on a tour?

00:09:19.689 --> 00:09:22.930
Jack: I would say, I would shut the door.

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:29.290
So the same is true for the story you I don't
worry about it it'll come through and anyone

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:34.249
who says you must know your story you might
if you want to put your money in a Uranium

00:09:34.249 --> 00:09:42.310
mine that's that's your business but you know
it's not what I teach.

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:46.850
So I'm not saying my way is the only way but
it's what I teach.

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:47.329
Question For The Viewers: Do you personally
write better with or without an outline?

